Glucagonoma
Endocrine & Metabolic Disorders
Necrolytic migratory erythema, Unexplained weight loss, New-onset diabetes, Markedly elevated serum glucagon

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-013
Reason for Visit: Severe, weeping, and painful skin rash, combined with acute exacerbation of chronic watery diarrhoea and profound weakness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, sibling
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a rapidly spreading, painful, blistering rash in a highly debilitated patient with new-onset severe diarrhoea and an inability to maintain oral hydration. Immediate out-of-hours assessment was required to rule out acute severe drug reaction (e.g., Stevens-Johnson Syndrome) or severe secondary skin infection, preventing systemic sepsis before standard clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My skin is peeling off in painful, weeping blisters, my mouth burns so badly I can't eat, and I'm losing weight rapidly."
History of Present Illness: Over the past two weeks, the patient developed a progressive, painful, pruritic erythematous rash with central blistering and crusting, predominantly affecting the perioral area, groin, and lower extremities. Tonight, the pain from the rash became unbearable, preventing sleep or mobility. This is set against a three-month history of severe unexplained unintentional weight loss (15 kg) and chronic debilitating watery diarrhoea. The patient also complains of a painful, smooth, red, and swollen tongue (glossitis) and angular cheilitis, severely limiting oral intake. They report profound generalized fatigue, depression, and recent new-onset glucose intolerance noted by their regular GP.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to severe pain from groin and lower extremity blistering and profound weakness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of severe dehydration and malnutrition. Sibling is unable to manage the complex wound care required.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 97 percent on room air | Wt: 55 Kg (down from 70 Kg)
General: Cachectic, chronically ill-appearing, in obvious discomfort from mucocutaneous lesions.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals classic necrolytic migratory erythema: widespread, painful, erythematous plaques with central bullae, crusting, and shedding, highly concentrated in the intertriginous areas and distal extremities. Oral exam shows a beefy red, smooth tongue with fissured, macerated labial commissures. Abdominal exam is soft with hyperactive bowel sounds. Tachycardia noted on cardiovascular exam. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: Necrolytic migratory erythema, Unexplained weight loss, New-onset diabetes (historical), and anticipated Markedly elevated serum glucagon pending hospital admission.
Environmental Observations: Bed linens are stained with serous exudate from the blistering rash.
Medication Reconciliation: Recently commenced on oral hypoglycaemics by GP. Denies any new antibiotics or allopurinol that might trigger SJS.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Glucagonoma with severe Necrolytic Migratory Erythema - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Stevens-Johnson Syndrome / Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronic, relapsing-remitting nature of the rash over weeks, the prominent glossitis, and the absence of a clear inciting pharmacological trigger.

Severe Zinc Deficiency (Acrodermatitis enteropathica) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of new-onset diabetes and massive, unexplained systemic weight loss pointing toward a paraneoplastic endocrine syndrome, though the rashes appear similar.

Disseminated Herpes Simplex Virus - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The specific migratory, crusting plaque morphology rather than grouped vesicles on an erythematous base, and the negative history of previous severe herpetic outbreaks.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute crisis of severe necrolytic migratory erythema, profound cachexia, and debilitating glossitis, highly indicative of a glucagon-secreting neuroendocrine tumour. Triage accurately recognized the severity of the blistering rash and the inability to maintain oral intake as high-risk for dehydration and secondary sepsis. Objective assessment confirmed the extent of the skin breakdown and the patient's cachectic, tachycardic state. Deferring care until standard business hours risked overwhelming secondary bacterial infection of the open lesions and severe metabolic decline. Immediate out-of-hours intervention was clinically mandated to provide systemic analgesia and coordinate acute medical admission, satisfying urgent MBS billing criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral oxycodone 5mg for severe cutaneous and mucosal pain. Applied sterile, non-adherent dressings to the most heavily exudative groin lesions. Arranged urgent ambulance transfer to the nearest tertiary hospital for intravenous hydration, nutritional support, and formal endocrine/oncology workup.
Positive Behavioral Change: By allowing the paramedics to transfer you smoothly using a slide sheet rather than trying to walk (action), you avoid applying shearing forces to your fragile skin (cause), which directly prevents further painful blistering and skin tearing (effect).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nil. Requires acute tertiary hospital admission.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Clinical handover provided to the receiving emergency department and oncology registrar on call.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance transfer arranged. If the patient develops a sudden, severe shortness of breath or sharp chest pain (indicating a high risk of pulmonary embolism associated with this condition), immediately inform the paramedics or dial 000 if waiting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The specific pattern of this rash combined with your weight loss and high blood sugar strongly suggests a rare hormone-secreting tumor of the pancreas. This requires complex hospital scans to confirm.
Clinical Course: Without specialized medical and surgical intervention to remove or suppress the tumor, the skin breakdown, weight loss, and diarrhoea will continue to relentlessly progress.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to apply any over-the-counter creams or ointments to the open sores as this may introduce infection. Wait comfortably for the ambulance.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A specific hormone is likely being overproduced by your body, which is directly destroying the top layer of your skin and causing you to rapidly burn through your nutritional stores. The hospital will focus on healing the skin, replacing your nutrition intravenously, and locating the source of the hormone.
Actionable Steps: Rest quietly. Do not attempt to eat or drink anything acidic or rough that will further damage your sore mouth.

